the alizay family from afghanistan is  having to move  along with another thousand refugees  they're being forced to leave the home  they've known for almost two years  at the karate bear community instead  they're being put in a tent city down  the road called mavrovunyo  their 17 year old daughter anise is a  gifted mathematician  who beat local greek students for a  place at the island's top high school  now she doesn't know if she'll be able  to sit university entrance exams next  year  a tent that does not have a door does  not make you feel safe  in karatepay we have a container and you  can close it when you go to bed at night  they have covered 19 cases which we  don't have at karatepay  and i think that will make greek parents  worry about  us travelling from the camp to go to  school with their children  this afghan family already lives at  motherhood  they say there's no daycare or school  for their daughters aged  six and nine doctors without borders say  they've had patients raped at the new  camp  despite government assurances that it's  safe in the night time  you feel too scared to go outside of  your tents  certainly if you're a woman you will not  go to the toilet every aspect of this  camp  for me is set up in a way  to to deter people  to to dehumanize them to to predict that  they don't have basic human needs  the government didn't allow us to film  inside the camp apparently due to covert  restrictions  but an aid worker confirmed talk of  discomfort here  there's no proper drainage there's no  proper heating there's  the electricity is insufficient to keep  your tents warm at night  every time it rains it floods the  wash facilities are inadequate it's  not clean these are the very problems  that plagued moria  the largest camp in the east aegean  mavrovonio was set up as a temporary  facility  after maurya burnt down in september  police say due to arson  now it feels like a lost civilization  looted for manholes and wire fence  garatepe feels more like a village  community than a refugee camp  and that's why so many disabled and  other vulnerable refugees  were moved there from moria the hastily  landscaped artillery range  they're going to resembles moria in many  respects  aid groups say it's a great step  backwards  the european union has poured more than  a billion dollars into refugee care in  greece in the past five years  and is now spending more than 300  million dollars to build  better camps on the aegean islands many  of these asylum seekers now displaced  several times over  wonder why europe with such resources  cannot offer them a home  john cerropolos al jazeera lesbos
 

